// Test: bugfixes/sem001_number_not_assignable_to_integer
// Grammar: assignment_statement, return_statement, function_call
// Semantic: type-system.md §5 Assignability — `integer` widens to `number`
//   implicitly; a `number` is never implicitly an `integer` (assignment,
//   argument or return).
// Fixed in: compiler 0.33.174
// Expected output:
// Expected error:
//   A value of type number cannot be used as integer without an explicit conversion
//   sem001_number_not_assignable_to_integer.cln:21:
//   sem001_number_not_assignable_to_integer.cln:22:
//   sem001_number_not_assignable_to_integer.cln:27:
//
// Regression: integer and number unified both ways, so `x = n`, passing 3.7
// to an integer parameter (invalid WASM) and `return 2.5` from an integer
// function were accepted.

start:
	number n = 2.5
	integer x = 1
	x = n
	printl(half(3.7).toString())
	number widened = x

functions:
	integer half(integer v)
		return 2.5
